// Allocators (design.md "Allocators", "Build-selected providers").
//
// M6a installs one provider: the system heap, reached through
// `mem.default_allocator()`. Source code cannot replace it; a future build
// option may choose another implementation without changing the handle ABI,
// because everything a caller holds is one pointer to one provider record.
//
// M6b adds the two local region roots, `mem.Arena` and `mem.Scratch`, which is
// what makes a `free_all` able to succeed at all.
@(public)
package mem;

// The compiler contributes:
//
//   Allocator          the pointer-sized provider handle
//   Allocator_Error    the allocation-failure result
//   default_allocator  the build-selected default provider
//
// `default_allocator` is the same symbol the generated default argument of a
// lifecycle hook names, so an omitted allocator and a written
// `mem.default_allocator()` select one provider through one call.
//
//   Arena              a local bump region, optionally over a caller's buffer
//   Scratch            the same, always provider-backed
//
// Both are compiler-owned because the region lattice has to *recognise* them,
// not merely call them: a region created here is one this body may reset without
// a promise, and one no owner may outlive.
//
//   arena := mem.Arena();               // blocks from the default provider
//   arena := mem.Arena(buffer[:]);      // the caller's storage, no allocation
//   scratch := mem.Scratch();
//   xs: [dynamic]int via arena.allocator() = {};
//   free_all(arena.allocator());        // reusable: the region stays usable
//
// A provider is move-only: two owners of one control block would release it
// twice, and a bump region has no meaningful copy. Moving one never changes its
// record address, so its region identity survives the move.
